          "answer": "1. **Partial Double Bond Character:** Delocalization of lone-pair electrons on the halogen into the aromatic ring creates resonance structures giving the $\\text{C-X}$ bond partial double bond character in haloarenes.\n2. **Difference in Carbon Hybridization:** Haloalkanes have $sp^3$-hybridized carbon (longer $\\text{C-X}$ bond, lower bond dissociation energy), while haloarenes have $sp^2$-hybridized carbon with 33.3% s-character (shorter, stronger $\\text{C-X}$ bond).\n3. **Steric and Electrostatic Shielding:** Approaching nucleophiles face electronic repulsion from the aromatic $\\pi$-electron cloud."
